WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.015 --> 00:00:09.465
Diabetes is an illness that has been known for thousands of years, and for most of history, people diagnosed with diabetes have suffered short and painful lives.

2
00:00:09.467 --> 00:00:15.076
After its symptoms appeared, the patient would invariably die, usually within a year.

3
00:00:15.077 --> 00:00:21.045
It causes blindness, nerve damage, and gangrene, which can lead to the loss of limbs.

4
00:00:21.046 --> 00:00:28.828
Early physicians knew that sugar was somehow involved because patients with diabetes produced urine with a strange, sweet smell.

5
00:00:28.829 --> 00:00:38.505
For thousands of years, the suffering of diabetics went unchecked, until a small-town doctor in Canada thought of a new approach to studying the disease.

6
00:00:38.506 --> 00:00:42.537
Dr. Frederick Banting was born on a farm near Alliston, Ontario.

7
00:00:42.538 --> 00:00:50.424
In 1920, he had a small medical practice in London, Ontario, but was teaching classes to earn some supplementary income.

8
00:00:50.425 --> 00:00:54.487
Doctors of his time knew that the pancreas was linked to diabetes.

9
00:00:54.488 --> 00:01:00.537
Experiments had shown that dogs whose pancreases had been surgically removed died from diabetes.

10
00:01:00.538 --> 00:01:06.204
They knew that something essential must be present in the pancreas, but finding it was frustrating.

11
00:01:06.205 --> 00:01:12.970
A healthy pancreas, it was believed, released a substance to deal with the sugar our bodies absorb when we eat.

12
00:01:12.971 --> 00:01:22.360
One evening, when preparing a lecture for his class, it occurred to Banting that the pancreas of a diabetic might destroy this substance before it can be used by the body.

13
00:01:22.361 --> 00:01:30.216
At two o'clock in the morning, he jotted down a quick note. It read, "Ligate pancreatic ducts of dogs."

14
00:01:30.217 --> 00:01:37.884
Banting's idea was to close off the small passages in a dog's pancreas that release its products into the rest of its body.

15
00:01:37.885 --> 00:01:40.996
His idea led to the discovery of insulin.

16
00:01:40.997 --> 00:01:45.321
Banting took his theory to the University of Toronto's physiology department.

17
00:01:45.322 --> 00:01:58.920
John R. Macleod, the head of the department, was skeptical of Banting's ideas, but nevertheless gave Banting laboratory space, ten laboratory dogs, and an assistant, a medical student named Charles Best.

18
00:01:58.921 --> 00:02:05.088
Banting tested his idea by performing surgery on some of the dogs to tie off the ducts in the pancreas.

19
00:02:05.089 --> 00:02:09.406
That way, any insulin they produced would remain inside the organ.

20
00:02:09.407 --> 00:02:15.638
After several weeks, Banting removed the pancreases and ground them in a solution of salt water.

21
00:02:15.639 --> 00:02:22.011
When this solution was injected into a diabetic dog, its symptoms improved almost instantaneously.

22
00:02:22.012 --> 00:02:29.677
Our bodies get energy by converting the carbohydrates in sweet or starchy foods into a particular sugar called glucose.

23
00:02:29.678 --> 00:02:37.781
But despite hours between meals and the occasional dessert loaded with sugar, the level of glucose in our blood stays relatively constant.

24
00:02:37.782 --> 00:02:44.218
It is insulin, a hormone produced in the pancreas, that regulates the amount of glucose in the blood.

25
00:02:44.219 --> 00:02:50.377
But diabetics either don't produce insulin or their bodies do not react to the insulin in their blood correctly.

26
00:02:50.378 --> 00:03:01.320
In a healthy person, insulin removes the excess blood sugar by storing it in the kidneys, as well as in muscle and fat cells, from where it can be released as needed.

27
00:03:01.321 --> 00:03:08.692
When a diabetic consumes food, the amount of sugar in the blood rises to toxic levels, damaging the body's tissues.

28
00:03:08.693 --> 00:03:17.311
The insulin Banting injected into the diabetic dog was all it needed to improve its health. The result was confirmed in other dogs.

29
00:03:17.312 --> 00:03:26.954
After this success, Macleod decided he would turn his attention to the project as well, and he brought in an expert in biochemistry to help, James Collip.

30
00:03:26.955 --> 00:03:31.303
Now that a team of four was assembled, new discoveries came quickly.

31
00:03:31.304 --> 00:03:37.287
They found that it wasn't necessary to ligate a dog's pancreatic ducts in order to collect insulin.

32
00:03:37.288 --> 00:03:42.002
With the proper technique, insulin could be extracted from a healthy pancreas.

33
00:03:42.003 --> 00:03:47.114
With the help of Collip, the team was able to purify the insulin they extracted.

34
00:03:47.115 --> 00:03:55.056
In 1922, their insulin extract was tried for the first time on a human patient, a 14-year-old boy named Leonard Thompson.

35
00:03:55.057 --> 00:04:00.341
Leonard was desperately sick with diabetes, but the treatment had a remarkable effect.

36
00:04:00.342 --> 00:04:08.931
His symptoms improved, but it was too early to celebrate. Leonard Thompson suffered a severe allergic reaction to the insulin extract.

37
00:04:08.932 --> 00:04:15.633
Although Banting and his colleagues had treated the boy's diabetes, they had also exposed him to unwanted chemicals.

38
00:04:15.634 --> 00:04:21.982
While Banting and Best searched for a better source of insulin, Macleod and Collip worked on its purification.

39
00:04:21.983 --> 00:04:26.573
Banting and Best had demonstrated that diabetes could be successfully treated.

40
00:04:26.574 --> 00:04:33.842
Turning a laboratory experiment into a treatment for an illness found all over the globe, however, was another matter entirely.

41
00:04:33.843 --> 00:04:41.016
One problem was collecting enough insulin. Cows and pigs were being slaughtered by the thousands in Toronto's abattoirs.

42
00:04:41.017 --> 00:04:44.794
Banting thought they might be a good and abundant source of the material.

43
00:04:44.795 --> 00:04:51.501
Experiments on cows and pigs were successful, meaning that both animals could be sources of insulin for diabetics.

44
00:04:51.502 --> 00:05:00.059
Banting and Best worked very hard to improve their formula, but they were both aware that while they were studying the problem, diabetics around the world were dying.

45
00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:08.881
Both realized that only a large pharmaceutical company had the resources needed to produce insulin extract in massive quantities and quickly purify it.

46
00:05:08.882 --> 00:05:17.944
Banting wanted insulin to be affordable and available to everyone who needed it, so he sold the commercial rights to insulin to the University of Toronto for a single dollar.

47
00:05:17.945 --> 00:05:26.301
The university then negotiated a deal with Eli Lilly and Company, an American pharmaceutical company, to mass-produce insulin in North America.

48
00:05:26.302 --> 00:05:31.688
Within one year of its discovery, insulin went from a laboratory experiment to a medicine.

49
00:05:31.689 --> 00:05:36.919
Insulin is not a cure for diabetes. It is, however, an effective treatment.

50
00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:43.503
With regular injections and a carefully managed diet, diabetics now look forward to longer, healthier lives.

51
00:05:43.504 --> 00:05:53.442
All four men played significant parts in creating a treatment for diabetes, but when the discovery of insulin was first reported, newspapers credited Macleod alone.

52
00:05:53.443 --> 00:05:59.657
Although Macleod corrected them and explained Banting's role, the event led to discord between the two men.

53
00:05:59.658 --> 00:06:06.394
When the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine was announced in 1923, Macleod and Banting were honored.

54
00:06:06.395 --> 00:06:13.378
Banting shared his prize money with Best, whom he considered his real partner, and Macleod shared his with Collip.

55
00:06:13.379 --> 00:06:18.819
Later, in 1934, Dr. Banting was knighted and became Sir Frederick Banting.

56
00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:23.375
For many years, the insulin extract used to treat diabetics came from animals.

57
00:06:23.376 --> 00:06:30.696
Some people, however, are allergic to the insulin that animals produce because it is slightly different from that produced by humans.

58
00:06:30.697 --> 00:06:39.848
With the advent of genetic engineering, it has become possible to change the DNA of small bacteria so that they produce insulin identical to that of humans.

59
00:06:39.849 --> 00:06:46.020
Today, it is estimated that over 175 million people worldwide have diabetes.

60
00:06:46.021 --> 00:06:52.574
Thanks to the work of a small-town doctor in Ontario, Canada, diabetes is no longer a death sentence.

61
00:06:52.575 --> 00:06:57.921
Now, people diagnosed with this disease can live long and healthy lives.

